WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.151
Mangroves are forests that grow along the coast, where land meets the sea.

00:00:06.651 --> 00:00:10.113
They help protect the coast from strong waves and storms,

00:00:10.113 --> 00:00:11.146
reduce soil loss,

00:00:11.146 --> 00:00:11.936
store carbon,

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:15.520
and provide a safe place for young fish and other sea life.

00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:19.564
But mangroves are being lost in many places.

00:00:19.564 --> 00:00:21.246
And when mangroves are lost,

00:00:21.246 --> 00:00:25.511
the remaining forests can become smaller and separated from each other.

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:29.698
A normal map can show us where the mangroves are.

00:00:30.198 --> 00:00:35.469
But it does not tell us which areas are important for keeping the whole mangrove system connected.

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:39.289
And it does not show us what could happen if an important area is lost.

00:00:41.689 --> 00:00:43.752
This is where EcoConnectAI comes in.

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:49.769
It takes the mangrove map one step further and helps us understand how the different areas are connected.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:51.550
First,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.403
we use satellite images to look at the coast,

00:00:54.403 --> 00:00:57.764
even when clouds make normal images difficult to use.

00:00:58.614 --> 00:01:02.352
Then AI identifies the areas that are likely to be mangroves.

00:01:03.202 --> 00:01:08.221
Next, we turn those areas into separate mangrove patches and connect them like a network.

00:01:08.221 --> 00:01:11.491
This helps us find which patches are especially important.

00:01:12.342 --> 00:01:15.511
We can then ask, “What happens if this patch is lost?

00:01:15.511 --> 00:01:17.067
” and simulate the result.

00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:22.604
We can also find possible places where new mangroves could help reconnect the network.

00:01:22.604 --> 00:01:24.675
These are suggestions from the system,

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:28.273
and would need to be checked on the ground before any real action.

00:01:29.673 --> 00:01:32.324
All of this comes together in one dashboard.

00:01:32.324 --> 00:01:33.771
You can explore the map,

00:01:33.771 --> 00:01:35.097
see important patches,

00:01:35.097 --> 00:01:36.423
run what-if scenarios,

00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:38.110
look at restoration options,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.014
create reports,

00:01:39.014 --> 00:01:41.968
and ask the built-in assistant about the project.

00:01:43.368 --> 00:01:48.703
EcoConnectAI is designed to support forest officers, conservation teams, and researchers.

00:01:48.703 --> 00:01:49.842
The goal is simple:

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:55.476
give them useful information that can help them understand the area and make better decisions.

00:01:56.877 --> 00:01:59.873
Today, EcoConnectAI is a working research prototype.

00:01:59.873 --> 00:02:02.813
It has been set up for four coastal areas in India.

00:02:02.813 --> 00:02:06.386
Its results are compared with an existing global mangrove map,

00:02:06.386 --> 00:02:09.729
but the system has not yet been tested with field surveys.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:14.715
The next step is to test the system in the field with partners.

00:02:14.715 --> 00:02:16.992
We also want to track changes over time,

00:02:16.992 --> 00:02:18.359
use more satellite data,

00:02:18.359 --> 00:02:23.540
and add information such as land and cost details to make restoration planning more useful.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.438
EcoConnectAI is more than a map.

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:29.990
It helps us understand where mangroves are,

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.354
how they are connected,

00:02:31.354 --> 00:02:33.075
which areas may be important,

00:02:33.075 --> 00:02:36.161
and what could happen if those connections are lost.

00:02:36.161 --> 00:02:39.484
EcoConnectAI — understand what holds the coast together.
